<<<
prohibit. The modern construc-

tion, apart from that with an object

noun as in an Act prohibiting export,

is from doing, not to do; the OED

marks the latter as archaic, but it

is less archaism than ignorance of

idiom & the analogy of forbid that

accounts forit in such contexts as:—
===
**prohibit.** The modern construction, apart from that with an object noun as in *an Act prohibiting export,* is *from* doing, not *to* do; the OED marks the latter as archaic, but it is less archaism than ignorance of idiom & the analogy of *forbid* that accounts for it in such contexts as:—
>>>

<<<
Marshal Oyama prohibited his troops

to take quarter within the walls./The

German Government has decided to

issue a decree prohibiting all Govern-

ment officials to strike. P. makes -tor; see -OR.
===
*Marshal Oyama prohibited his troops to take quarter within the walls.*/*The German Government has decided to issue a decree prohibiting all Government officials to strike.* *P.* makes *-tor*; see ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
prohibition. Pronounce préji- ; the h is sounded, however, where the i fallowing it bears the accent, as in prohibit itself. See PRONUNCIATION.
===
**prohibition.** Pronounce prōĭ-; the *h* is sounded, however, where the *i* following it bears the accent, as in *prohibit* itself. See ^^PRONUNCIATION^^.
>>>

<<<
project. Verb projé’kt, noun pro’jikt; see Noun & VERB ACCENT. The verb makes -tor; see -or.
===
**project.** Verb projĕ'kt, noun prŏ'jĭkt; see ^^NOUN & VERB ACCENT^^. The verb makes *-tor*; see ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
prolate, -lative. See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**prolate, -lative.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
prolegomena. A plural, of which the sing., rarely used, is -menon.
===
**prolegomena.** A plural, of which the sing., rarely used, is *-menon*.
>>>

<<<
prolepsis. See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**prolepsis.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
prolific is in common use, but to make a satisfactory noun from it has passed the wit of man. Provificacy, prolificalness, prolificity, & prolificness, have been tried & found wanting; tility, productiveness, Sruttfulness, are the best solution.
===
**prolific** is in common use, but to make a satisfactory noun from it has passed the wit of man. *Prolificacy, prolificalness, prolificity,* & *prolificness,* have been tried & found wanting; substitutes such as *fertility, productiveness, fruitfulness,* are the best solution.
>>>

<<<
prologue, -logize, -loguize. The prevalent modern pronunciation is pro‘lég, but the OED gives preference to prd‘l6g. In the verb it seems best to spell -gize, the Greeks having the verb -gizo, but with licence at least te GREEK G) to pronounce it pro’logiz.
===
**prologue, -logize, -loguize.** The prevalent modern pronunciation is prō'lŏg, but the OED gives preference to prŏ'lŏg. In the verb it seems best to spell *-gize*, the Greeks having the verb *-gizo*, but with licence at least (see ^^GREEK G^^) to pronounce it prŏ'logīz.
>>>

